      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a19.5.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g19.5.9', 'g19.5.12', 'g19.5.17'],
          lemma: "Ἰδοὺ ὁ ἄνθρωπος",
          title: "“Behold the man!”",
          body: "Pilate means the words as a plea for pity — *look at this pathetic, beaten figure; surely he is no threat, surely this is enough.* The scourging alone could kill a man; on top of it the soldiers have mocked the “king” with a crown that draws blood and a cast-off officer’s cloak dyed the imperial purple. **“Behold the man!”** — see, he is only a broken human being, let it end here. But John, who loves to record words that say more than the speaker knows, wants us to hear the deeper truth. *Behold the man* — behold **the Man**, the true and representative human being, the Second Adam, standing where our whole race stands: condemned, mocked, crowned with the thorns that are the curse of a fallen world (Genesis 3:18). The soldiers meant the crown and robe as cruel parody; they were more right than they knew, for this *is* the King, and this — the suffering, the shame, the thorns — is precisely *how* he reigns. The world looks and sees a defeated man; faith looks at the same figure and sees the King of glory being enthroned. And there is a summons in it for every reader: behold him. Do not look away. This is what your salvation cost, and this is the face of the God who loves you.",
        },
      ],
