      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a19.22.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g19.19.19', 'g19.20.23', 'g19.22.6'],
          lemma: "Ὃ γέγραφα, γέγραφα",
          title: "The King in three languages",
          body: "Pilate, out of spite or superstition or the last defiance of a man who has already caved, nails a placard over the cross: **“Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews”** — and refuses to change a word of it when the priests object: *“What I have written, I have written.”* He does not know what he is doing. He means it as mockery — *here is your king, a bleeding criminal* — but John sees the deeper truth: the charge nailed over the cross is the plain fact. This *is* the King, and the cross is exactly where and how he reigns. And notice the three languages — **Aramaic, Latin, and Greek** — the tongue of revelation, the tongue of empire, and the tongue of culture; the whole world of that day is summoned to read the proclamation. The gospel that will go to every nation is announced, unwittingly, over the dying Christ, in the languages of all who pass by. Even the enemies of Jesus become heralds of his crown. Nothing at the cross is out of his Father’s hands — not even the sign a pagan governor wrote to insult him.",
        },
      ],
