      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a19.30.1',
          kind: "reading",
          refs: ['g19.30.9', 'g19.30.14', 'g19.28.9'],
          lemma: undefined,
          title: "“It is finished”",
          body: "One word in the Greek — **τετέλεσται**, *tetelestai* — and it is not the cry of a victim but the shout of a victor. It is not *“I am finished”* (defeat) but *“It is finished”* (completion). The word was written across paid bills — *paid in full* — and stamped on tasks brought to their end. The debt of sin owed by the human race, the work the Father gave the Son to do (17:4), the whole long anticipation of the Law and the Prophets: all of it, here, *accomplished.* Nothing is left to add; salvation is not begun and left for us to complete, but *done.* Note how John frames the death, and how it differs from a mere execution. Jesus is not overpowered; twice John says he acts in full knowledge and control — *knowing that all was now finished* (v. 28) — and then **“he bowed his head and gave up his spirit.”** The order is telling: a dying man’s head drops and then he expires; here Jesus first *bows* his head, as if lying down to rest, and then *hands over* his spirit — the same active verb used of a deliberate gift. No one takes his life; he lays it down (10:18). Even his last breath is an act of sovereign self-offering. The King, having finished the work of love, dismisses his own spirit and dies as he lived — in command. And the finished work stands forever: there is nothing left for anyone to do but receive it.",
        },
      ],
