      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a19.34.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g19.34.9', 'g19.34.13', 'g19.34.15'],
          lemma: "αἷμα καὶ ὕδωρ",
          title: "Blood and water",
          body: "A soldier, making sure of death, drives a spear into Jesus’ side, and John records what he saw with unusual emphasis: **“at once there came out blood and water,”** and *“he who saw it has borne witness… that you also may believe”* (v. 35). Why does it matter so? First, plainly: it is proof that Jesus *really died* — a genuine corpse, not a swoon; the separation of blood and serum is what a real death and a real spear-thrust produce. The resurrection will be no resuscitation of a man who never quite died. But John, always working on two levels, surely intends more. Blood and water are the two great cleansing streams of his Gospel and letters: **blood** for atonement, the life poured out that covers sin; **water** for the new birth and the Spirit (3:5; 7:38–39), the cleansing that renews. From the pierced side of the Second Adam — as Eve was drawn from the side of the first — the church is born, washed and redeemed. And the moment fulfills two ancient words at once: *“not one of his bones will be broken”* (the Passover lamb, Exodus 12; Psalm 34:20) — for he is the true Passover, dying as the lambs are slain — and *“they will look on him whom they pierced”* (Zechariah 12:10). The soldier’s spear, meant only to confirm a death, becomes a sign: here is the fountain opened for sin and uncleanness, flowing from the heart of the crucified.",
        },
      ],
